• Voltage and current:
  Chips use milliamps, motors use hundreds of milliamps, and far more when stalled.

• Common ground: Every GND in the mouse must connect together. 
  A signal is only "3.3 V" relative to ground, so a missing ground causes random, confusing behaviour.

• 3.3 V logic: ESP32 pins output 3.3 V and are damaged by 5 V. 
  The simple rule: power every sensor and both encoders from the ESP32's 3.3 V pin, so no signal wire can ever carry more than 3.3 V.

• Digital pins. A pin is HIGH (3.3 V) or LOW (0 V). The motor driver's DIR pins use this to pick direction.

• PWM. Switching a pin on and off very fast, here 20,000 times a second. 
  The fraction of time it is on sets the average power, so 50% on means roughly half speed. You write it as 0–255.

• I2C. A two-wire bus (SDA = data, SCL = clock) shared by many devices, each with an address. 
  All three VL53L0X sensors start at the same address, 0x29, so your code must give them new addresses at every power-up.
  The IMU sits at 0x68.

• Interrupts. Encoders send thousands of pulses a second. An interrupt makes the ESP32 run a tiny function on every pulse so none are missed. 
  Variables changed inside an interrupt must be declared volatile.


A short circuit is battery + touching battery − directly; with a lithium battery that means heat, smoke or fire.


Every part below runs from 3.3 V except the motor driver's motor supply and the ESP32's 5 V input. 
Check each board's printed labels on the day; wire colours vary between batches, labels don't.

ESP32-C6 (the brain)
• A 160 MHz microcontroller programmed over USB from the Arduino IDE. Its pins are 3.3 V and NOT 5 V tolerant.
• Avoid on most C6 boards: GPIO8, GPIO9 and GPIO15 (boot pins), GPIO12 and GPIO13 (USB), GPIO16 and GPIO17 (serial). 
  Confirm against your board's own pinout diagram.
• The BOOT button is usually GPIO9. Once your program is running you can read it as your run-mode button.

DFRobot DRI0044 motor driver (TB6612FNG chip)
• Each motor needs two signals: DIR (HIGH or LOW sets direction) and PWM (sets speed). That's four ESP32 pins in total. DFRobot wiki
• Power pins: VCC is logic power (2.7–5.5 V), so connect it to 3.3 V. VM is motor power (2.5–12 V), from the battery; see section 5.
• Outputs: M1+/M1− to the left motor, M2+/M2− to the right. 1.2 A continuous per channel is plenty for N20 motors.
• A motor spinning the wrong way is fixed by swapping its two wires or flipping its DIR in code. Nothing breaks.

2× GA12-N20 motors with Hall encoders
• Each has a 6-wire cable: two motor wires (to the driver), encoder VCC, encoder GND, and encoder outputs A and B. 
  Read the labels on the little encoder board; never assume by colour.
• Rated 6 V and about 500 rpm with no load at the output shaft.
• The encoder is a magnet on the motor shaft passing two Hall sensors. 
  A and B pulse slightly out of step, so the order they change tells you direction.
• Power encoder VCC from 3.3 V so A and B are 3.3 V signals.
• Don't calculate counts per wheel turn from the datasheet. Measure it: turn the wheel 10 times by hand and divide the count by 10.

3× VL53L0X distance sensors (left, front, right)
• A laser time-of-flight sensor that reports distance in millimetres. 
  Expect a few millimetres of noise and a fixed offset that you measure and subtract.
HOW TO FIND OFFSET:
  1. Put the sensor facing a large, flat wall.
  2. Measure the actual distance with a ruler/caliper.
  3. Take many VL53L0X readings.
  4. Average them.
  5. offset = sensor reading − actual distance
• Pins: VIN (to 3.3 V), GND, SDA, SCL, XSHUT (shutdown), GPIO1 (leave unconnected).
• All three wake up at I2C address 0x29. Your setup code holds all three off with XSHUT (Like an on and off switch controlled by the ESP32)
  wakes one, renames it, then the next. 
  This must happen at every power-up.
CODE SHOULD LOOK SOMETHING LIKE THIS on every power-up:
void setup() {
    // Configure XSHUT pins
    // Turn all sensors OFF
    leftOff();
    frontOff();
    rightOff();

    // Start LEFT
    leftOn();
    left.setAddress(0x30);

    // Start FRONT
    frontOn();
    front.setAddress(0x31);

    // Start RIGHT
    rightOn();
    right.setAddress(0x32);
}

• The beam is a cone about 25° wide, which is why a badly angled side sensor sees the floor or over the wall top.
  Don't assume the sensor measures along one infinitely thin straight ray. It has a relatively wide field of view.
  Mechanical alignment of the VL53L0Xs is part of the sensor calibration, not just an aesthetics
HOW TO TEST:
1. Start with one sensor, Have it continuously print:
    distance: 103 mm
    distance: 104 mm
    distance: 102 mm
    distance: 103 mm
    ...
  You want a simple way to see the raw readings.

2. Test the basic accuracy
   Put the sensor on a table and put a large, flat, light-coloured wall/object in front of it.
   Start at something like 100 mm.
   Measure the actual distance with a ruler:

    Sensor ●────────────────│ wall
           <---- 100 mm ---->
    
    Then collect 50–100 readings.
    Calculate the average.
    
    Eg:
    Actual distance:     100 mm
    Average reading:     104 mm
    Offset = 104 - 100
           = +4 mm

3. Repeat with several distances

4. Now test the 25° field of view
    Rotate sensor and see at which angle it behaves insensibly.


SAFETY:
• Never connect USB and the external 5 V at the same time. 
  Make the buck-to-ESP32 5 V connection one removable jumper wire: pull it before plugging in USB.
• For bench testing with Serial Monitor: 
  USB powers the ESP32, the battery still powers VM, and the 5 V jumper is out. 
  Grounds stay joined through the driver.
• Before the first power-up, use the multimeter's beep (continuity) mode between the 5 V and GND rails, 
  then 3.3 V and GND. A beep means a short: find it before connecting power.
• For the first 5 seconds of any new power-up, watch and sniff. 
  Anything hot or a burning smell: unplug the battery immediately.
• Unplug the battery whenever you solder or rewire.
• Test motors with the wheels off the table (mouse sitting on a roll of tape), 
  so it can't drive off the bench.
• A swollen, dented or hot battery gets handed to the organisers, not used.




Here's the wiring order, safest first. Keep the battery unplugged until step 7.

1
Get the answers first
Ask the organisers what they approve for the 6 V motors on an 8.4 V battery, and which ESP32-C6 board you have. Then fill every GPIO blank in the wiring table and pins.h before picking up an iron.
2
Solder the header pins
Solder headers onto the ESP32, motor driver, IMU and the three VL53L0X boards. Beep-test each pin with the multimeter so it connects to its pad and not to its neighbour.
3
Set the buck to 5.00 V
With nothing on its output, connect the battery to IN+ and IN−, turn the trim screw until OUT reads 5.00 V, then disconnect the battery.
4
Build the power and ground rails
Join every GND together. Run battery + (through the switch) to the driver's VM and the buck's IN+. Run buck OUT+ to the ESP32's 5V pin through one removable jumper. Take the ESP32's 3V3 pin out as your 3.3 V rail.
5
Wire the motors and driver
Driver VCC to 3.3 V. DIR1, PWM1, DIR2 and PWM2 to their GPIOs. M1+/M1− to the left motor's two motor wires, M2+/M2− to the right.
6
Wire the encoders
Each encoder's VCC to 3.3 V (never 5 V) and GND to ground. Its A and B outputs go to their GPIOs. Read the labels on the encoder board rather than trusting wire colours, and keep these wires away from motor wires.
7
Wire the I2C sensors
One SDA wire and one SCL wire shared by all three VL53L0X boards and the IMU. Every sensor's power goes to 3.3 V and GND. Each VL53L0X's XSHUT goes to its own GPIO; leave GPIO1 unconnected. Label them L, F, R.
8
Check, then power up
Beep-test 5 V to GND and 3.3 V to GND: a beep means a short, so find it first. Then connect the battery with the wheels off the table, and watch for heat or smell for 5 seconds. Run Tests 1–4 to confirm each part works.

The rule behind all of this: only the driver's VM and the ESP32's 5V pin ever see more than 3.3 V, and you never plug in USB while the 5 V jumper is connected. The wiring table in section 6 of the guide has every pin row to tick off as you go.
